+## Duration
+
+{class}`click.Duration` converts a string to a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. Accepted formats:
+
+- A whole number of seconds made only of ASCII digits, e.g. `120` (120 seconds) or `0`.
+- One or more `<integer><unit>` groups with units `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m` (minutes) and
+  `s` (seconds). Each unit may appear at most once and must appear in that order. Units are
+  case-insensitive and no spaces are allowed, e.g. `2d`, `1h30m`, `45S`, `1d2h3m4s`, `90m`, `1H30M`.
+
+Any other input (negative values, decimals, empty strings, unknown units, repeated or out-of-order
+units, whitespace) fails with `'<value>' is not a valid duration.`
+
+```python
+@click.command()
+@click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="1h30m")
+def cli(timeout):
+    click.echo(timeout.total_seconds())
+```
+
